```{r setup_knitr, include=FALSE}
library('BiocStyle')
knitr::opts_chunk$set(autodep = TRUE, cache = FALSE, cache.lazy = FALSE, dev = 'png',
  fig.path = "figure/${short_tag}_train_${ref_tag}.Rmd/", message = FALSE, warning = FALSE)
knitr::opts_knit$set(root.dir = '..', base.dir = '../public/', base.url = '')
```

```{r setup_libs, include=FALSE}
source('code/utils.R')
source('code/train_xgboost.R')
source('code/marker_genes.R')
```

```{r setup_input, include=FALSE}
predictions_f          = "${predictions_f}"
gain_f                 = "${importance_f}"
model_card_f           = "${model_card_f}"
pb_f                   = "${pseudobulk_f}"
label_counts_f         = "${label_counts_f}"
fulldata_predictions_f = "${fulldata_predictions_f}"
depth_evaluation_f     = "${depth_evaluation_f}"
integration_f          = "${integration_f}"
min_cells              = as.integer("${min_cells}")
n_cores                = as.integer("${n_cores}")
setDTthreads(n_cores)

# Read saved predictions and coordinates; no model fitting or inference is run.
preds_dt = fread(predictions_f)
gain_dt = fread(gain_f)
feature_selection = read_xgboost_feature_selection(model_card_f)
model_configuration = jsonlite::fromJSON(model_card_f)$configuration
depth_dt = read_xgboost_depth_evaluation(depth_evaluation_f)
int_dt = fread(integration_f, select = c('cell_id', 'UMAP1', 'UMAP2'))
label_order = fread(label_counts_f)[order(-len)]$label
style = read_xgboost_style(predictions_f, label_order)
label_order = style$order
has_coarse = length(style$mapping) > 0
```

```{r make_xgboost_dt, include=FALSE}
xgboost_dt = merge(preds_dt, int_dt, by = 'cell_id', all.x = TRUE)[!is.na(UMAP1)]
xgboost_dt = apply_xgboost_style(xgboost_dt, style)
val_dt = xgboost_dt[split == 'validation']

# Each row is one integrated cell, including cells omitted by the sampling caps.
fulldata_dt = fread(fulldata_predictions_f)
fulldata_dt[!selected & split %in% c('train', 'validation'), split := 'development_holdout']
fulldata_dt = merge(fulldata_dt, int_dt, by = 'cell_id', all.x = TRUE)[!is.na(UMAP1)]
fulldata_dt = apply_xgboost_style(fulldata_dt, style)
```

## Cells used for training and validation

Training cells are used to fit the classifier. Validation cells come from separate groups, such as tissue tubes or donors, and are used to decide when to stop training. The left panel shows the density of both groups together; the right shows the percentage assigned to validation in each hexagon. Uneven coverage can reflect differences in the size and composition of the groups held aside.

```{r plot_train_test_split, fig.height=6, fig.width=13}
plot_train_test_split(xgboost_dt)
```

## Predicted vs reference labels UMAP {.tabset}

Compare the supplied reference labels with predictions for validation cells. These cells were used to decide when to stop training, so agreement here can overestimate performance on a new dataset.

```{r plot_pred_true_umap, fig.height=7, fig.width=14, results='asis'}
for (coarse in c(FALSE, if (has_coarse) TRUE)) {
  cat('\n\n###', if (coarse) 'Coarse' else 'Labels', '\n\n')
  print(plot_pred_true_umap(val_dt, coarse = coarse,
    label_colours = if (coarse) style$coarse_colours else style$colours))
}
```

## Confusion matrices {.tabset}

Rows are reference labels and columns are predictions. Colours show the proportion of each reference label assigned to each prediction, on a log scale so smaller fractions remain visible. A strong diagonal indicates agreement; off-diagonal entries identify labels to examine.

Performance on cells used to train or tune the classifier can be optimistic. Test cells, when specified, come from groups held aside from both steps and provide a more independent check. Remaining cells were left out to limit training and validation cell counts, but may share donors or samples with those used for training.

When supplied, coarse labels combine the standard labels according to the specified mapping. The same mapping is applied to reference labels and predictions.

```{r confusion_matrix, fig.height=10, fig.width=12, results='asis'}
for (coarse in c(FALSE, if (has_coarse) TRUE)) {
  cat('\n\n###', if (coarse) 'Coarse' else 'Labels', '{.tabset}\n\n')
  reference_col = if (coarse) 'coarse_true' else 'label'
  plot_order = if (coarse) style$coarse_order else label_order
  for (sp in names(XGBOOST_CELL_GROUPS)) {
    cat('\n\n####', XGBOOST_CELL_GROUPS[sp], '\n\n')
    cells_dt = fulldata_dt[split == sp & !is.na(get(reference_col))]
    if (nrow(cells_dt)) {
      print(plot_xgboost_confusion(cells_dt, plot_order, coarse = coarse))
    } else {
      cat('No annotated cells in this group.\n\n')
    }
  }
}
```


## Evaluation metrics by label {.tabset}

N is the number of reference cells for each label. Precision asks: of cells predicted to have this label, what fraction match the reference? Recall asks: of cells with this reference label, what fraction were recovered? F1 combines precision and recall, penalising a low value in either. Compare training, validation and test results separately, using the distinction described above.

```{r metrics_table, results='asis'}
for (coarse in c(FALSE, if (has_coarse) TRUE)) {
  cat('\n\n###', if (coarse) 'Coarse' else 'Labels', '{.tabset}\n\n')
  reference_col = if (coarse) 'coarse_true' else 'label'
  plot_order = if (coarse) style$coarse_order else label_order
  for (sp in names(XGBOOST_CELL_GROUPS)) {
    cat('\n\n####', XGBOOST_CELL_GROUPS[sp], '\n\n')
    cells_dt = fulldata_dt[split == sp & !is.na(get(reference_col))]
    if (nrow(cells_dt)) {
      metrics_dt = get_metrics_dt(cells_dt, coarse = coarse)
      metrics_dt[, Class := factor(Class, levels = plot_order)]
      cat(format_xgboost_metrics_table(metrics_dt[order(Class)]), '\n\n')
    } else {
      cat('No annotated cells in this group.\n\n')
    }
  }
}
```


## Precision–recall (validation set) {.tabset}

Each point represents one reference label; size shows its cell count. The zoom uses the minimum precision or recall across labels as the lower limit on both axes.

```{r pr_plot_validation, fig.height=7, fig.width=12, results='asis'}
for (coarse in c(FALSE, if (has_coarse) TRUE)) {
  cat('\n\n###', if (coarse) 'Coarse' else 'Labels', '{.tabset}\n\n')
  plot_order = if (coarse) style$coarse_order else label_order
  plot_colours = if (coarse) style$coarse_colours else style$colours
  metrics_dt = get_metrics_dt(val_dt, coarse = coarse)
  metrics_dt[, Class := factor(Class, levels = plot_order)]
  for (zoom in c(FALSE, TRUE)) {
    cat('\n\n####', if (zoom) 'Zoomed axes' else 'Full axes', '\n\n')
    print(plot_precision_recall(metrics_dt, plot_colours, zoom = zoom))
  }
}
```


## Full-data evaluation

Predictions cover all cells in the integration, including cells omitted from the sampled fitting data:

* **Train:** selected cells used to fit the model.
* **Validation:** cells from separate groups, used to decide when to stop training.
* **Test:** cells from groups held aside from both model fitting and the decision about when to stop training.
* **Remaining cells:** annotated cells omitted by the training/validation sampling caps; these are not independent test cells.
* **Excluded labels:** reference labels deliberately excluded from fitting and primary evaluation.
* **Insufficient training support:** reference labels with too few training cells.
* **Unlabelled cells:** cells without reference annotations.

### Evaluation summary {.tabset}

Accuracy is the fraction of cells whose predicted and reference labels agree. Macro F1 gives each label equal weight when averaging its F1 score, so abundant labels do not dominate. These measures assess agreement with the supplied annotations, which may themselves contain errors. The test row is absent when no test groups were held aside. Interpret small populations alongside their cell counts and donor/region coverage.

```{r evaluation_summary, results='asis'}
for (coarse in c(FALSE, if (has_coarse) TRUE)) {
  cat('\n\n####', if (coarse) 'Coarse' else 'Labels', '\n\n')
  evaluation_f = if (coarse) {
    sub('_predictions.csv.gz$', '_coarse_evaluation.csv', predictions_f)
  } else {
    sub('_fulldata_predictions.csv.gz$', '_evaluation.csv', fulldata_predictions_f)
  }
  if (file.exists(evaluation_f)) {
    evaluation_dt = fread(evaluation_f)
    evaluation_dt[, split := XGBOOST_SPLIT_LABELS[split]]
    cat(knitr::kable(evaluation_dt, format = 'html', digits = 3), '\n\n')
  }
}
```


### Cell group summary

```{r fulldata_split_summary, results='asis'}
split_counts_dt = fulldata_dt[, .N, by = split][order(-N)]
split_counts_dt[, split := XGBOOST_SPLIT_LABELS[split]]
cat(knitr::kable(split_counts_dt, format = 'html'), '\n\n')
```

## Full-data predicted labels UMAP {.tabset}

Predicted labels for each cell group. Excluded and unlabelled cells are shown separately from the evaluated annotated groups. Coarse calls use the same direct label mapping as the metrics and confusion matrices.

```{r fulldata_umap, fig.height=9, fig.width=12, results='asis'}
for (coarse in c(FALSE, if (has_coarse) TRUE)) {
  cat('\n\n###', if (coarse) 'Coarse' else 'Labels', '{.tabset}\n\n')
  plot_colours = if (coarse) style$coarse_colours else style$colours
  for (sp in names(XGBOOST_SPLIT_LABELS)) {
    cells_dt = fulldata_dt[split == sp]
    if (!nrow(cells_dt) && !sp %in% names(XGBOOST_CELL_GROUPS)) next
    cat('\n\n####', XGBOOST_SPLIT_LABELS[sp],
      sprintf('(%s cells)', format(nrow(cells_dt), big.mark = ',')), '\n\n')
    if (nrow(cells_dt)) {
      print(plot_xgboost_prediction_umap(cells_dt, plot_colours, coarse = coarse))
    } else {
      cat('No cells in this group.\n\n')
    }
  }
}
```

## Accuracy at lower sequencing depth {.tabset}

Classifiers trained on deeply sequenced cells can misclassify shallow cells, because marker genes go undetected and single reads of other genes appear highly expressed. To show how this classifier behaves, up to 200 cells per label with more than 2,000 UMIs are randomly thinned to fixed depths, keeping each read with equal probability, and classified again. Cells never used for fitting are chosen first (test cells, then remaining cells). Labels without enough of them, such as rare labels whose cells were all needed for fitting, are topped up with validation and then training cells; the model has seen those cells, so their accuracy is optimistic. The same cells are used at every depth, so differences reflect depth rather than cell composition.

Accuracy that stays close to the full-depth value down to the depths of the data you plan to classify indicates a robust model. A steep fall at low depths means shallow cells will often be mislabelled. The overall curve uses only cells never used for fitting. In the by-label plot, labels whose accuracy drops below 90% at any depth are coloured and named, and dashed lines mark labels evaluated partly or wholly on fitted cells; the confusion matrix shows which labels absorb the misclassified cells. Thinning removes reads at random, so real shallow libraries can perform somewhat differently, and remaining cells come from the same donors and samples as the training cells. `r if (isTRUE(model_configuration$downsample_copies > 0)) sprintf("This model was trained with %d thinned %s of each training and validation cell, at %s to %s UMIs (\x60downsample_copies\x60, \x60downsample_min_umis\x60, \x60downsample_max_umis\x60).", model_configuration$downsample_copies, if (model_configuration$downsample_copies == 1) "copy" else "copies", format(model_configuration$downsample_min_umis, big.mark = ","), format(model_configuration$downsample_max_umis, big.mark = ",")) else "If accuracy falls at the depths you need, retrain with depth augmentation (\x60downsample_copies: 1\x60), choosing \x60downsample_min_umis\x60 and \x60downsample_max_umis\x60 to cover those depths."`

```{r depth_accuracy_overall, fig.height=5, fig.width=8, results='asis'}
if (nrow(depth_dt)) {
  cat('\n\n### Overall\n\n')
  print(plot_depth_accuracy(depth_dt))
} else {
  cat('No unused annotated cells had more than 2,000 UMIs, so accuracy at lower depth could not be estimated.\n\n')
}
```

```{r depth_accuracy_labels, fig.height=6, fig.width=9, results='asis'}
if (nrow(depth_dt)) {
  cat('\n\n### By label\n\n')
  print(plot_depth_accuracy_by_label(depth_dt, style$colours))
}
```

```{r depth_confusion, fig.height=10, fig.width=12, results='asis'}
