  shallow_depth = levels(depth_dt$depth)[2]
  cat('\n\n### Confusion matrix at', shallow_depth, 'UMIs\n\n')
  print(plot_xgboost_confusion(depth_dt[depth == shallow_depth], label_order))
}
```


## Cumulative gain curve

Genes are ranked by how much they helped distinguish the reference labels during training, measured by XGBoost gain. High gain indicates usefulness to this classifier; it does not establish that a gene is a specific biological marker. The horizontal line shows the requested `r scales::percent(feature_selection$gain_threshold, accuracy = 1)` cumulative gain target. The vertical line shows the actual number of genes included in the final model, after applying the minimum and maximum gene counts.

```{r plot_gain_curve, fig.height=5, fig.width=8}
plot_gain_curve(gain_dt, gain_threshold = feature_selection$gain_threshold,
  n_selected_genes = feature_selection$n_selected_genes)
```

## Top XGBoost genes {.tabset}

Expression of genes used by the final classifier, ranked by their contribution during training. Counts are summed within each label and batch, normalised for library size and shown as logCPM. Each dot represents one batch; its size indicates the number of contributing cells. Check that useful genes show biologically plausible expression rather than patterns confined to one batch.

```{r load_cpms, include=FALSE}
pb = read_h5ad(pb_f, as = 'SingleCellExperiment')
if ('X' %in% assayNames(pb)) assays(pb)[['X']] = NULL
batch_col = intersect(c('batch', 'sample_id'), colnames(colData(pb)))[1]
cpms_dt = make_logcpms_all_rmd(pb, batch_col, lib_size_method = 'edger',
  min_cells = min_cells, n_cores = n_cores)
```

```{r plot_top_genes, fig.height=8, fig.width=8, results='asis'}
top_genes_dt = gain_dt[gene_id %in% cpms_dt$gene_id][order(-gain)][1:min(100, .N)]
per_page = 10
for (i in seq(1, nrow(top_genes_dt), by = per_page)) {
  cat('\n\n### Page', ceiling(i / per_page), '\n\n')
  selected_genes_dt = top_genes_dt[i:min(i + per_page - 1, .N)]
  print(plot_top_xgboost_genes(selected_genes_dt, cpms_dt,
    label_order = label_order, label_colours = style$colours))
}
```


## Top genes by label {.tabset}

Genes contributing most to each label's predictions in the final model. A multi-class classifier builds separate trees for each label, so a gene's gain is summed over that label's trees; the bracket shows the label's running cumulative gain. These are the genes the model relies on to recognise each label, which can include genes that are low in that label but high in labels it must be distinguished from. Expression is shown as for the overall top genes.

```{r plot_top_genes_by_label, fig.height=8, fig.width=8, results='asis'}
label_gain_f  = sub('_gene_importance.csv$', '_gene_importance_by_label.csv', gain_f)
label_gain_dt = fread(label_gain_f)
for (lbl in intersect(label_order, unique(label_gain_dt$label))) {
  cat('\n\n###', lbl, '\n\n')
  selected_genes_dt = label_gain_dt[label == lbl & gene_id %in% cpms_dt$gene_id][order(-gain)][1:min(10, .N)]
  print(plot_top_xgboost_genes(selected_genes_dt[, .(gene_id, gain, cumulative_gain_frac)], cpms_dt,
    label_order = label_order, label_colours = style$colours))
}
```
